polar-coordinates needs complex-plane: locates a point of the plane by distance r and angle θ
exponential-function needs power-series: e^z is defined by the series Σ zⁿ/n!
exponential-function needs circle-of-convergence: the exponential series has infinite radius, converging everywhere
complex-logarithm needs logarithm: extends log's product-to-sum rule to the complex plane
complex-logarithm needs exponential-function: log is the inverse of e^z, which repeats with period 2πi
complex-logarithm needs polar-coordinates: log w = log r + iθ, its imaginary part the argument
euler-identity needs exponential-function: setting z = πi in e^z gives −1
euler-formula needs exponential-function: splitting e^(iθ) into real and imaginary parts gives cos θ + i sin θ
euler-formula needs polar-coordinates: lets z be written in polar form as r e^(iθ)
complex-powers needs complex-logarithm: w^z is defined as e^(z log w), needing a chosen branch
equiangular-spiral needs complex-powers: the many values of w^z lie where equiangular spirals intersect
roots-of-unity needs complex-powers: the n values of 1^(1/n) are complex powers of 1
roots-of-unity needs euler-formula: e^(2πik/n) places them as a regular n-gon on the unit circle
cyclic-group needs roots-of-unity: the nth roots of unity form Z_n under multiplication
multiplicative-quantum-numbers needs roots-of-unity: their values are nth roots of unity
multiplicative-quantum-numbers needs additive-quantum-numbers: combine by products over constituents as additive ones do by sums
parity needs multiplicative-quantum-numbers: parity is the n = 2 case, ε = ±1
parity needs antiparticles: for odd parity a particle's mirror image is its antiparticle
quarkiness needs multiplicative-quantum-numbers: Penrose's n = 3 case, taking values 1, ω, ω²
quarkiness needs cyclic-group: quarkiness values 1, ω, ω² form the group Z₃
quarkiness needs electric-charge-quantization: defined as e^(−2qπi) from third-integer quark charges; free particles give 1
q-why-only-plane-rotations relates complex-multiplication-geometry: is about: multiplying by a unit complex number rotates a plane
q-why-only-plane-rotations relates q-why-nature-uses-complex-numbers: extends: asks what plays the role of complex numbers in 3D
complex-powers relates q-complex-power-paradox: resolves: fixing a branch of log makes powers single-valued
q-complex-power-paradox relates complex-logarithm: turns on: log is defined only up to multiples of 2πi
complex-powers relates q-i-to-the-i: answers: i^i = e^(i log i) = e^(−π/2), real with infinitely many values

