continuity needs function-as-mapping: asks whether a mapping's graph has jumps
derivative needs function-as-mapping: the limiting slope of chords on a function's graph
derivative needs continuity: differentiability needs continuity; |x| is continuous yet fails at its corner
higher-derivatives needs derivative: differentiate repeatedly; zeros of f' and f'' give extrema and inflections
smooth-functions needs higher-derivatives: C^n counts how many derivatives exist and are continuous
smooth-functions needs continuity: C^0 is plain continuity, the base of the smoothness scale
taylor-series needs power-series: a power series built from a function at a point
taylor-series needs higher-derivatives: coefficients are a_n = f^(n)(p)/n!
analytic-functions needs taylor-series: analytic means equal to its Taylor series near every point
analytic-functions needs smooth-functions: C^ω is stronger than C^∞; exp(−1/x) glued to 0 is smooth, not analytic
analytic-functions relates q-what-is-a-function: addresses: analyticity rules out gluing, matching Euler's single-formula notion
leibniz-rule needs derivative: rules like d(fg) = f dg + g df make differentiating formulas mechanical
integration needs derivative: integration undoes differentiation, up to a constant
fundamental-theorem-of-calculus needs derivative: area under f equals g(b) − g(a) for any g with g' = f
fundamental-theorem-of-calculus needs integration: links definite integrals as areas to indefinite integrals as antiderivatives
dirac-delta-function needs heaviside-step-function: the delta function is the derivative of the step function
dirac-delta-function needs derivative: differentiating the step's jump yields an infinite spike at the origin
dirac-delta-function needs smooth-functions: extends the C^n scale below zero: delta is C^−2
q-what-is-a-function relates function-as-mapping: contrasts with: Euler's single unglued function versus an arbitrary look-up table
q-what-is-a-function relates smooth-functions: tests: whether C^∞ is enough, but smooth pieces can still be glued
q-differentiation-vs-integration relates leibniz-rule: involves: these rules make differentiating explicit formulas routine
q-differentiation-vs-integration relates integration: is about: integrals of formulas often need new functions, yet numerics favour integration
q-differentiation-vs-integration relates smooth-functions: involves: integrating raises the C^n class by one, differentiating lowers it

